WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.770 --> 00:00:05.020
So good morning.

00:00:05.020 --> 00:00:08.029
Everyone 9 AM on the West Coast.

00:00:08.029 --> 00:00:10.580
I'm Alex Bui, Co-director of the BD2K Centers
Coordination Center.

00:00:10.580 --> 00:00:14.960
So today, we continue our computing overview
with what I believe is our fifth talk in this

00:00:14.960 --> 00:00:15.960
section.

00:00:15.960 --> 00:00:20.160
I have the distinct pleasure of introducing
Dr. Vivien Bonazzi of NIH.

00:00:20.160 --> 00:00:24.550
Dr. Bonazzi is the Senior Adviser for Data
Science Technologies and the Commons, which

00:00:24.550 --> 00:00:26.140
is what she'll be talking about shortly.

00:00:26.140 --> 00:00:31.700
She serves on the BD2K executive committee
and leads a variety of data science and sustainability

00:00:31.700 --> 00:00:34.140
efforts through the National Institutes of
Health.

00:00:34.140 --> 00:00:39.150
She holds a PhD in molecular pharmacology
and computational biology from the University

00:00:39.150 --> 00:00:44.560
of Melbourne and was a senior associate at
Booz Allen Hamilton, managing several genome

00:00:44.560 --> 00:00:46.460
and protein bioinformatics projects there.

00:00:46.460 --> 00:00:51.900
As part of NIH, she has served as the program
director for the National Human Genome Research

00:00:51.900 --> 00:00:58.300
Institute and was part of the Human Microbiome
Project, where she was responsible for the

00:00:58.300 --> 00:01:01.570
bioinformatic and computational elements of
that effort.

00:01:01.570 --> 00:01:03.780
So please join me in welcoming Dr. Bonazzi
this morning.

00:01:03.780 --> 00:01:07.060
She'll be giving us some insights about the
relatively new and exciting NIH Commons.

00:01:07.060 --> 00:01:10.340
So Dr. Bonazzi, thank you for joining us today.

00:01:10.340 --> 00:01:11.840
The floor is yours.

00:01:11.840 --> 00:01:12.840
Right.

00:01:12.840 --> 00:01:13.860
Good morning, everyone.

00:01:13.860 --> 00:01:15.369
Thank you, Alex.

00:01:15.369 --> 00:01:20.560
Thank you for the opportunity to speak to
you today.

00:01:20.560 --> 00:01:24.240
So today, I'm going to talk to you a little
bit about the Commons, but also just give

00:01:24.240 --> 00:01:31.299
you a few updates on where we are with that,
which is the context of this talk today.

00:01:31.299 --> 00:01:36.499
So I'm just going to-- can everyone see my
screen?

00:01:36.499 --> 00:01:43.439
Is the answer to that yes, Alex, to you, at
least?

00:01:43.439 --> 00:01:45.079
Hello?

00:01:45.079 --> 00:01:49.450
Can you hear me?

00:01:49.450 --> 00:01:56.159
Alex, can you hear me?

00:01:56.159 --> 00:01:58.789
Hello?

00:01:58.789 --> 00:01:59.850
We can hear you.

00:01:59.850 --> 00:02:00.850
OK, great.

00:02:00.850 --> 00:02:02.989
I'm just trying to get my slides to advance.

00:02:02.989 --> 00:02:08.860
All right, so the previous slide was just
a little bit about myself.

00:02:08.860 --> 00:02:09.860
Let's keep moving.

00:02:09.860 --> 00:02:12.800
So I'm going to talk to you a little bit about
biomedical big data.

00:02:12.800 --> 00:02:18.130
I don't know what all of the audience's background
is, so I'm going to assume something in biomedical

00:02:18.130 --> 00:02:21.810
areas and some different degrees in terms
of data science.

00:02:21.810 --> 00:02:26.190
And I'm going to try and build up a picture
of, essentially, why we would need something

00:02:26.190 --> 00:02:29.730
like the Commons, and how we're going to approach
it in NIH.

00:02:29.730 --> 00:02:35.500
So starting, really, at its basic level, this
is the kind of data that NIH is well known

00:02:35.500 --> 00:02:36.500
for.

00:02:36.500 --> 00:02:37.500
A lot of it is-
So starting, really, at its basic level, this

00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:38.500
is the kind of data that NIH is well known
for.

00:02:38.500 --> 00:02:41.050
A lot of it is-a lot of it was analog, but
it's moving more and more to digital sciences.

00:02:41.050 --> 00:02:45.560
And I think we see that from some of the examples
I'm just showing here.

00:02:45.560 --> 00:02:48.980
And as we get more towards wearables and collecting
that kind of information, we're obviously

00:02:48.980 --> 00:02:54.750
going towards more and more data collection
as well.

00:02:54.750 --> 00:03:00.190
So I think this slide is something that, maybe,
people have seen before, which is, we hear

00:03:00.190 --> 00:03:02.790
about the four Vs related to big data.

00:03:02.790 --> 00:03:04.240
I've heard of several different other ones.

00:03:04.240 --> 00:03:09.090
And that is the Volume, as in the amount;
the Velocity, which is the speed at which

00:03:09.090 --> 00:03:14.300
you need to process this information, and
it's coming up very quickly; the Variety,

00:03:14.300 --> 00:03:18.910
in terms of the different data types that
we have; and then the Veracity, which is really

00:03:18.910 --> 00:03:22.060
the trustworthiness of the data and its cleanliness.

00:03:22.060 --> 00:03:23.960
Can you really trust it, and can you use it?

00:03:23.960 --> 00:03:27.230
These are the ones that I've seen a lot of,
and I've seen a few others.

00:03:27.230 --> 00:03:32.319
But one of the ones that I want to focus on
today is also this, which is that I think

00:03:32.319 --> 00:03:34.150
that data has value.

00:03:34.150 --> 00:03:38.330
And it's, I think, a signal of the coming
digital economy that I think we're all realizing

00:03:38.330 --> 00:03:46.120
we're in, and that data is really central
to that particular digital economy.

00:03:46.120 --> 00:03:50.150
I think that economy is characterized by using
data to gain a business advantage.

00:03:50.150 --> 00:03:55.940
And I think, yes, institutions, not just commercial
entities, are businesses when we come to data,

00:03:55.940 --> 00:03:59.709
and I think that organizations that aren't
born digital will be at a disadvantage in

00:03:59.709 --> 00:04:05.200
that new economy.

00:04:05.200 --> 00:04:09.940
So I think organizations will be defined by
their digital assets in the future.

00:04:09.940 --> 00:04:15.319
And by digital assets, I mean these, in terms
of the sciences-- that is, data, software,

00:04:15.319 --> 00:04:19.549
workflows, documentations, journal articles,
and there's probably others here, as well,

00:04:19.549 --> 00:04:20.549
that I could include.

00:04:20.549 --> 00:04:24.560
But I think these are the ones that most people
would know about.

00:04:24.560 --> 00:04:28.539
And I think what's really important here is
that a lot of the most successful organizations

00:04:28.539 --> 00:04:33.430
of the future will be those that can leverage
those digital assets and, importantly, transform

00:04:33.430 --> 00:04:35.660
them into a digital enterprise.

00:04:35.660 --> 00:04:39.430
How do we pull all these pieces together,
is the question we have to ask.

00:04:39.430 --> 00:04:42.199
We know we have the pieces, but what do we
need to do?

00:04:42.199 --> 00:04:47.069
I think one of the key ones is to make data
the central currency of an organization.

00:04:47.069 --> 00:04:52.180
And it's really usable in a digital ecosystem,
in the context of where we're talking about

00:04:52.180 --> 00:04:53.180
the Commons.

00:04:53.180 --> 00:04:57.159
So really, the transactions of using data
is really what the Commons facilitates.

00:04:57.159 --> 00:05:02.990
And I'm going to go through that in a little
more detail now.

00:05:02.990 --> 00:05:06.789
So let's have a look a little bit about the
problem with biomedical data.

00:05:06.789 --> 00:05:10.270
And when I talked about digital assets before,
I want to say that includes data.

00:05:10.270 --> 00:05:12.930
It isn't just data.

00:05:12.930 --> 00:05:17.759
And I think there are lots of
challenges in biomedical data, and that is--

00:05:17.759 --> 00:05:20.569
and they fall in a couple of different categories.

00:05:20.569 --> 00:05:25.440
There is the journal article, which is still,
I think, the end goal of many scientists.

00:05:25.440 --> 00:05:31.020
And that's fine, but the data, which is seen
as a means to an end, is really low value.

00:05:31.020 --> 00:05:35.449
And I think the argument I was just making
before is that data actually has value, and

00:05:35.449 --> 00:05:39.469
it's higher than what we're giving it at the
moment.

00:05:39.469 --> 00:05:44.039
The data is not FAIR, and that is the term
that is being used fairly recently.

00:05:44.039 --> 00:05:48.710
I'm assuming that listeners have heard of
this term, but those that don't, I'm implying

00:05:48.710 --> 00:05:50.849
that it's Findable, Accessible, Interoperable,
and Reproducible.

00:05:50.849 --> 00:05:55.729
And there have been a number of papers over
the last few years which talk about this--

00:05:55.729 --> 00:06:00.229
that in fact, if we're going to give data
value, it has to have these properties here

00:06:00.229 --> 00:06:03.520
in order to be able to do something with it.

00:06:03.520 --> 00:06:07.030
I think there are also limited infrastructures
to support FAIR data.

00:06:07.030 --> 00:06:13.629
And I think what the Commons is trying to
do is going some way towards doing that.

00:06:13.629 --> 00:06:21.050
So here is just a short presentation, which
is from the New York Health Sciences Library.

00:06:21.050 --> 00:06:22.960
Some of you may have seen this-- it's quite
long.

00:06:22.960 --> 00:06:24.400
The original is actually quite long.

00:06:24.400 --> 00:06:29.490
I've just taken a short abstract from it to
sort of identify the problems with data.

00:06:29.490 --> 00:06:33.520
And this little YouTube video really encapsulates
the issues with data.

00:06:33.520 --> 00:06:37.900
I think it's only about a minute and a bit
long, so I'm going to play that now.

00:06:37.900 --> 00:06:43.210
The link to the full video shown here at the
bottom of the screen, so you can go to YouTube

00:06:43.210 --> 00:06:45.090
and actually have a look at that.

00:06:45.090 --> 00:06:46.090
So here it is.

00:06:46.090 --> 00:06:47.090
Enjoy.

00:06:47.090 --> 00:06:49.410
Hopefully the audio will play for you.

00:06:49.410 --> 00:06:50.660
[VIDEO PLAYBACK]
- Hi.

00:06:50.660 --> 00:06:51.660
- Hello.

00:06:51.660 --> 00:06:53.580
- My name is Dr. Judy Benign.

00:06:53.580 --> 00:06:57.300
I'm an oncologist at NYU School of Medicine.

00:06:57.300 --> 00:06:59.330
- Hello, Dr.
Judy Benign.

00:06:59.330 --> 00:07:01.279
- I read your article about B cell functions.

00:07:01.279 --> 00:07:03.919
It was very interesting.

00:07:03.919 --> 00:07:08.599
I think that I could use the data for my work
on pancreatic cancer.

00:07:08.599 --> 00:07:12.559
- I am not an oncologist.

00:07:12.559 --> 00:07:18.149
- I know, but I think I could use the data
for my work on pancreatic cancer.

00:07:18.149 --> 00:07:19.940
- Oh.

00:07:19.940 --> 00:07:21.499
- So do you have the data?

00:07:21.499 --> 00:07:23.869
- Everything you need to know is in the article.

00:07:23.869 --> 00:07:26.159
- No, what I need is the data.

00:07:26.159 --> 00:07:27.759
Will you
share your data?

00:07:27.759 --> 00:07:30.090
- I am not sure that will be possible.

00:07:30.090 --> 00:07:33.339
- But your work is in PubMed Central and was
funded by NIH.

00:07:33.339 --> 00:07:34.569
- That is true.

00:07:34.569 --> 00:07:38.899
- And it was published in Science, which requires
that you share your data.

00:07:38.899 --> 00:07:40.549
- I did publish in Science.

00:07:40.549 --> 00:07:44.000
- Then I am requesting your data.

00:07:44.000 --> 00:07:45.749
Can I have a copy of your data?

00:07:45.749 --> 00:07:49.400
- Everything you need to know is in the article.

00:07:49.400 --> 00:07:51.279
- No.

00:07:51.279 --> 00:07:56.919
- I am not sure where my data is.

00:07:56.919 --> 00:07:58.689
- But surely you saved your data.

00:07:58.689 --> 00:07:59.689
- I did.

00:07:59.689 --> 00:08:01.799
I saved it on a USB drive.

00:08:01.799 --> 00:08:04.829
- Where is the USB drive?

00:08:04.829 --> 00:08:10.199
- It is in a box.

00:08:10.199 --> 00:08:11.740
It is in a box at home.

00:08:11.740 --> 00:08:14.409
I just moved.

00:08:14.409 --> 00:08:20.649
- But can I use your data?

00:08:20.649 --> 00:08:22.229
- There are many boxes.

00:08:22.229 --> 00:08:27.649
Well, I forgot to label the boxes.

00:08:27.649 --> 00:08:35.760
[END PLAYBACK]
And as you can see, I think this encapsulates

00:08:35.760 --> 00:08:38.430
a lot of the problems that we're seeing with
data.

00:08:38.430 --> 00:08:39.460
It's a small vignette.

00:08:39.460 --> 00:08:41.720
I highly recommend watching the entire video.

00:08:41.720 --> 00:08:43.710
It's in three parts.

00:08:43.710 --> 00:08:44.710
So what's changing?

00:08:44.710 --> 00:08:48.000
We know what the problems are, but let's have
a look at what's actually changing.

00:08:48.000 --> 00:08:52.630
I think one of the things I just mentioned
before, which is the issue about making data

00:08:52.630 --> 00:08:57.650
FAIR, which is Findable, Accessible, Interoperable,
and Reproducible, and I've just shown a clipping

00:08:57.650 --> 00:09:04.720
here from the [? Walkerson ?] paper that came
out about a year and a half-- year ago that

00:09:04.720 --> 00:09:05.930
actually starts addressing this.

00:09:05.930 --> 00:09:08.460
There have been a couple of additional papers
around this area.

00:09:08.460 --> 00:09:14.530
And I think what it's doing is really explaining
what you need to think about when you're dealing

00:09:14.530 --> 00:09:18.620
with data and the kinds of issues that you
need to be considering when you're dealing

00:09:18.620 --> 00:09:19.660
with data overall.

00:09:19.660 --> 00:09:25.100
And I think this is having an impact at all
sorts of different levels, from institutions

00:09:25.100 --> 00:09:30.110
like NIH, but I know it's also having an impact
across different government institutions,

00:09:30.110 --> 00:09:35.350
both in the United States, certainly in Europe,
also Australia, and in Asia.

00:09:35.350 --> 00:09:39.260
And I think what you're going to see is the
applications of these two data sets as we

00:09:39.260 --> 00:09:41.380
move forward.

00:09:41.380 --> 00:09:45.470
Another key issue that's, I think, changing,
that's hopefully avoiding what we just saw

00:09:45.470 --> 00:09:51.330
in that short video, is the data sharing policies
from funding agencies like NIH, but I think

00:09:51.330 --> 00:09:56.200
other agencies that also deal with data, which
is making the data shareable-- not just in

00:09:56.200 --> 00:10:01.080
a form, as in making data FAIR, but also having
policies saying we want things to be shared,

00:10:01.080 --> 00:10:07.120
looking at open data and the standards associated
with that, and the things that go along with

00:10:07.120 --> 00:10:14.200
grants that are actually paid by NIH, and
ensuring that that data is made available.

00:10:14.200 --> 00:10:19.030
But I think we're also seeing a couple other
things, and that is that when you store your

00:10:19.030 --> 00:10:24.780
data, that you're moving towards-- instead
of just having it on local repositories or

00:10:24.780 --> 00:10:28.970
in general archive repositories-- we're actually
looking at, now, research data repositories,

00:10:28.970 --> 00:10:31.410
which store data per se.

00:10:31.410 --> 00:10:34.700
And there are a number of these that have
been coming about, and I think that's also

00:10:34.700 --> 00:10:39.400
a change in the way that we're looking at
data, making these available in a fair format,

00:10:39.400 --> 00:10:43.560
but also having them available to the broader
community, rather than just a USB drive, which

00:10:43.560 --> 00:10:46.030
is what the previous YouTube video showed.

00:10:46.030 --> 00:10:49.151
I think the other thing is metadata.

00:10:49.151 --> 00:10:53.800
Metadata is really important in describing
the data that you have.

00:10:53.800 --> 00:10:58.230
And ensuring that it has quality standards
around it is really important.

00:10:58.230 --> 00:11:03.110
There have been many projects at NIH which
have used metadata, but not particularly well,

00:11:03.110 --> 00:11:04.600
or used community standards.

00:11:04.600 --> 00:11:08.810
And this is critical for the way that you
actually want to index this information, and

00:11:08.810 --> 00:11:13.300
search it, and make it findable by others
and usable by others.

00:11:13.300 --> 00:11:17.860
Another one which is-- another change that
I'm seeing is the use of the unique identifiers-ensuring

00:11:17.860 --> 00:11:24.870
that we have identifiers for the data per
se that are consistent and also to formulated

00:11:24.870 --> 00:11:25.870
standards.

00:11:25.870 --> 00:11:27.760
And standards are starting to come across
this area.

00:11:27.760 --> 00:11:31.620
So I think where the YouTube video showed
a lot of problems, I think here are some of

00:11:31.620 --> 00:11:35.430
the areas that I think we're starting to see
some change, which is starting to enable the

00:11:35.430 --> 00:11:38.410
better use of data and to see value in the
system.

00:11:38.410 --> 00:11:42.540
But I think we're still in the early days
of this, and I think it's important that it

00:11:42.540 --> 00:11:48.110
happens from the ground up, from the PhD students
all the way up to the leaders of the institutions,

00:11:48.110 --> 00:11:53.550
to embrace these kinds of changes and to enable
them within their organizations, or even at

00:11:53.550 --> 00:11:56.640
a small level, as graduate
students actually do this within their own

00:11:56.640 --> 00:12:00.990
work.

00:12:00.990 --> 00:12:04.330
So I think what we're also trying to get at
here is that the FAIR principles drive data

00:12:04.330 --> 00:12:05.740
to become the currency.

00:12:05.740 --> 00:12:11.190
If you actually can describe it, you know
what it is, it's interoperable, it's reproducible,

00:12:11.190 --> 00:12:15.160
it's basically an apples-to-apples comparison,
you can understand what it is that you have.

00:12:15.160 --> 00:12:19.430
And I think what we also need to look at are
policies that promote data sharing via FAIR,

00:12:19.430 --> 00:12:25.660
can really help change that culture, which
is why I think the leveraging of fair principles,

00:12:25.660 --> 00:12:30.440
and turning those into guidelines that the
community can use within the context of their

00:12:30.440 --> 00:12:36.530
own data, really will help promote this kind
of work.

00:12:36.530 --> 00:12:37.560
So that's great.

00:12:37.560 --> 00:12:38.620
You make data FAIR.

00:12:38.620 --> 00:12:43.420
But then I think what we also need is ecosystems
that allow transactions to occur on that FAIR

00:12:43.420 --> 00:12:44.420
data.

00:12:44.420 --> 00:12:48.000
And importantly for biomedical data, you want
to do this at scale.

00:12:48.000 --> 00:12:51.850
We all know, particularly from genome studies,
but I think also from some of the brain studies,

00:12:51.850 --> 00:12:56.800
where you're looking at not just genomic data,
but also imaging studies from those things,

00:12:56.800 --> 00:12:59.480
that the data is large and getting larger.

00:12:59.480 --> 00:13:03.360
And it's quite cheap to generate the data,
but it's a lot of effort on the other end

00:13:03.360 --> 00:13:08.639
to store it, and also to look at harmonization
and to deal with all of these FAIR concepts.

00:13:08.639 --> 00:13:12.490
So I think what we want to do is to do all
of these things, to look at the way we handle

00:13:12.490 --> 00:13:17.530
data in the future.

00:13:17.530 --> 00:13:20.170
So now I want to talk a little bit about the
Data Commons.

00:13:20.170 --> 00:13:23.250
Up until now, I've just explained the kinds
of things that we're seeing.

00:13:23.250 --> 00:13:27.100
I think much of the community knows about
big data, but I've tried to sort of lay the

00:13:27.100 --> 00:13:32.310
groundwork about the sort of pieces around
the data, the problems, and the things that

00:13:32.310 --> 00:13:33.310
are changing.

00:13:33.310 --> 00:13:37.880
As I just mentioned, what you also need to
think about is the way a platform works here

00:13:37.880 --> 00:13:39.990
to support this whole ecosystem.

00:13:39.990 --> 00:13:43.110
And really, the work that's going on in the
Data Commons is really that.

00:13:43.110 --> 00:13:47.590
It's looking at a platform that fosters the
development of a digital ecosystem to support

00:13:47.590 --> 00:13:53.650
transactions on FAIR data.

00:13:53.650 --> 00:13:57.620
So I'm just going to talk a little bit about
some of the principles behind the Commons,

00:13:57.620 --> 00:13:59.980
as we see it.

00:13:59.980 --> 00:14:05.130
And that is, as I just mentioned, it's a platform
that fosters development of a digital ecosystem.

00:14:05.130 --> 00:14:09.450
But it treats the products of research, whatever
they are, as digital assets.

00:14:09.450 --> 00:14:14.910
And as I mentioned before, these will be the
currencies of institutions in the future.

00:14:14.910 --> 00:14:19.180
We want all of these digital objects to conform
to FAIR principles, as I've just said before.

00:14:19.180 --> 00:14:21.620
And we
want these digital objects to exist in some

00:14:21.620 --> 00:14:28.650
sort of shared virtual space so that you can
find them, deposit, manage, share, reuse--

00:14:28.650 --> 00:14:33.810
essentially do work on these objects, and
do science.

00:14:33.810 --> 00:14:36.870
And you want to you create a platform, because
the platforms enable interactions between

00:14:36.870 --> 00:14:39.810
producers and consumers of those digital assets.

00:14:39.810 --> 00:14:43.980
And I think there are many biomedical scientists
who produce information that want it to be

00:14:43.980 --> 00:14:48.530
consumed by others, and what we want is some
sort of level exchange to do that.

00:14:48.530 --> 00:14:53.290
It also gives currencies to the digital assets,
and the people who develop and support them.

00:14:53.290 --> 00:14:58.920
One of the big issues is that the papers that
are produced can often have PIs, and that's

00:14:58.920 --> 00:15:03.560
important to do that, but a lot of the people
that do the data scrubbing, the informaticians,

00:15:03.560 --> 00:15:07.600
some of the software engineers, don't get
the kind of currency that they really need

00:15:07.600 --> 00:15:10.410
when they're actually working on these digital
assets.

00:15:10.410 --> 00:15:14.400
And I think papers are great, but it would
also just be very helpful, and I think really

00:15:14.400 --> 00:15:19.820
good, to give currency to the digital assets
themselves, be they-- whatever they are.

00:15:19.820 --> 00:15:24.380
And I think the most common ones right now
that people look at are data and software.

00:15:24.380 --> 00:15:27.520
And if somebody developed those, they get
attribution for that.

00:15:27.520 --> 00:15:30.510
And there is a way to do that that isn't necessarily
a paper.

00:15:30.510 --> 00:15:33.150
And I think we're starting to see elements
of that-- you see that, certainly, within

00:15:33.150 --> 00:15:38.210
GitHub, and you see it within data repositories
that are just involved in sorting data, particularly

00:15:38.210 --> 00:15:42.740
from papers.

00:15:42.740 --> 00:15:44.870
So I mentioned before that the Data Commons
is a platform.

00:15:44.870 --> 00:15:47.470
So I'd like to talk to you a little bit more
about a platform.

00:15:47.470 --> 00:15:51.990
And it's essentially a platform that fosters
that development of the ecosystem.

00:15:51.990 --> 00:15:54.630
So let's talk a little bit about what that
might look like.

00:15:54.630 --> 00:15:57.470
And we're in the fairly early days of this.

00:15:57.470 --> 00:16:01.470
So I'm going to take a quote here from a fellow
that I've been doing some work with, here,

00:16:01.470 --> 00:16:05.240
which is Sangeet Paul Choudary, who's been
looking at-- he's written a book called Platforms

00:16:05.240 --> 00:16:06.240
of Scale.

00:16:06.240 --> 00:16:07.670
This slide has a link to that.

00:16:07.670 --> 00:16:09.550
I presume these slides will be made public.

00:16:09.550 --> 00:16:13.630
I'll certainly put them on my SlideShare afterwards,
and you'll be able to download them.

00:16:13.630 --> 00:16:19.800
Sangeet looks at platforms in the digital
space, and I'll explain those in one minute.

00:16:19.800 --> 00:16:24.310
But here, he's saying, "Platforms is a plug
and play model allows multiple participants,

00:16:24.310 --> 00:16:28.600
the producers and consumers, to connect to
it, to interact with each other, and to create

00:16:28.600 --> 00:16:30.170
value."

00:16:30.170 --> 00:16:35.650
And what Sangeet just been looking at specifically
is all of these platforms, that if I just

00:16:35.650 --> 00:16:39.610
showed you this icon, you'll identify pretty
much all of them.

00:16:39.610 --> 00:16:42.310
A very classic one is AirBnB, right?

00:16:42.310 --> 00:16:45.279
It's a platform that allows producers and
consumers to interact

00:16:45.279 --> 00:16:52.529
with vacant apartments, houses, essentially
accommodation, with people who need that.

00:16:52.529 --> 00:16:57.040
And he's been looking at how those platforms
are set up to connect producers and consumers,

00:16:57.040 --> 00:17:03.070
and what really allows them to work, and what
are their business models behind that.

00:17:03.070 --> 00:17:06.650
So as I mentioned before, but I think it's
worth stating again, that you want interactions

00:17:06.650 --> 00:17:09.140
between producers and consumers.

00:17:09.140 --> 00:17:14.310
So somebody who generates biomedical data
and often the tools, but not necessarily just

00:17:14.310 --> 00:17:19.640
that, wants to be able to share that information
with others, either within a consortium or

00:17:19.640 --> 00:17:23.110
with just others in the future who want to
consume it-- say, if they have an new grant

00:17:23.110 --> 00:17:29.140
that they want to submit, or application they
want to submit to NIH.

00:17:29.140 --> 00:17:33.360
So to understand the Data Commons Platform,
and really, how it might work for biomedical

00:17:33.360 --> 00:17:38.440
sciences, I'm going to use what's called a
platform stack to help visualize this concept.

00:17:38.440 --> 00:17:42.370
Sangeet looks at it in terms of the broader
digital community, but I think it's really

00:17:42.370 --> 00:17:46.850
important for us to look at some of the layers
that are really consistent amongst every single

00:17:46.850 --> 00:17:52.270
one of these platforms that he's been talking
about, and certainly consistent with the ones

00:17:52.270 --> 00:17:55.720
I just showed before, and examples were Uber,
AirBnB, et cetera.

00:17:55.720 --> 00:17:57.200
They're really in three layers.

00:17:57.200 --> 00:18:01.900
There's a technology infrastructure component,
there's a data component, and there's a network

00:18:01.900 --> 00:18:05.840
marketplace community that consumes this information.

00:18:05.840 --> 00:18:12.170
In the context of the Data Commons for NIH,
this is something I've been working on over

00:18:12.170 --> 00:18:17.691
the last 18 months or so, and really looking
at, OK, how do we look at this-- if this is

00:18:17.691 --> 00:18:25.150
a platform to support data at-- for NIH data,
what's the right way to think about it?

00:18:25.150 --> 00:18:28.170
And this is something that's just a model
of that stack, and a diagram.

00:18:28.170 --> 00:18:31.330
I'll just walk through it just briefly, here.

00:18:31.330 --> 00:18:33.300
So the bottom layer is the technology layer.

00:18:33.300 --> 00:18:35.580
This is where we have a compute platform.

00:18:35.580 --> 00:18:38.530
You want to operate on the data in some form.

00:18:38.530 --> 00:18:44.130
We are going to leverage cloud services at
NIH, in part because we see a great deal of

00:18:44.130 --> 00:18:49.730
researchers, particularly using large data,
moving from repositories to clouds-- commercial

00:18:49.730 --> 00:18:55.690
clouds-- essentially doing some work on it,
some form of analysis, and once they've done

00:18:55.690 --> 00:18:59.010
their analysis, they remove it from the cloud,
and they have their results.

00:18:59.010 --> 00:19:03.000
But they generally don't leave their data
sets up there, because storage costs are too

00:19:03.000 --> 00:19:07.030
high, and certainly, moving multiple petabytes
of data would incur a huge number of costs

00:19:07.030 --> 00:19:08.030
for them.

00:19:08.030 --> 00:19:11.900
So we're looking at, what's the right way
to use the cloud platform to facilitate the

00:19:11.900 --> 00:19:13.720
researchers at NIH?

00:19:13.720 --> 00:19:16.630
In saying that, we're also dealing with supercompute
facilities.

00:19:16.630 --> 00:19:21.230
They're primarily funded through DOE, not
NIH, but we're working with them to figure

00:19:21.230 --> 00:19:22.270
out how we can collaborate.

00:19:22.270 --> 00:19:26.910
But essentially, bottom line is, you've got
a technology platform here, which is cloud

00:19:26.910 --> 00:19:27.910
supercompute.

00:19:27.910 --> 00:19:30.040
S
The next layer is data, and we have a collection

00:19:30.040 --> 00:19:35.990
of data that-- some of those are from large
generated data sets, which are funded by NIH.

00:19:35.990 --> 00:19:39.090
These are often-- I call them reference data
sets.

00:19:39.090 --> 00:19:45.140
They're in the, certainly, terabyte to petabyte
range, generated by NIH, usually under consortiums,

00:19:45.140 --> 00:19:47.470
and with the intent to share.

00:19:47.470 --> 00:19:51.440
And just some of the examples I can think
of, there are the encode data sets, which

00:19:51.440 --> 00:19:57.740
are used by the community, and also the GTEx
data sets, are some examples of ones that

00:19:57.740 --> 00:19:58.740
I've been dealing with.

00:19:58.740 --> 00:20:03.110
These are large, hundreds of terabyte, petabyte
data sets, which people want to consume, but

00:20:03.110 --> 00:20:08.960
they can't do that either locally, in their
own systems, or at the repositories, because

00:20:08.960 --> 00:20:11.400
they simply don't have the compute resources
to it.

00:20:11.400 --> 00:20:15.429
But we also have user-defined data, in the
sense that people have smaller data sets that

00:20:15.429 --> 00:20:19.090
they actually want to bring to these larger
data sets, perhaps, and actually do some sort

00:20:19.090 --> 00:20:20.090
of analysis.

00:20:20.090 --> 00:20:23.690
But the bottom layer is that you've got a
whole bunch of data which needs to be computed

00:20:23.690 --> 00:20:25.220
against.

00:20:25.220 --> 00:20:30.590
And then the top layer here is really saying
that if you have a platform with data, you

00:20:30.590 --> 00:20:32.630
want to be able to do various things to it.

00:20:32.630 --> 00:20:36.750
And the most common thing that people see
is, you want to do some sort of analysis on

00:20:36.750 --> 00:20:38.160
it-- scientific analysis.

00:20:38.160 --> 00:20:42.230
But to do that in these environments, you
also need to have a collection of services

00:20:42.230 --> 00:20:43.230
to enable you to do that.

00:20:43.230 --> 00:20:46.790
First of all, you need to find this information,
so you need some for of indexing.

00:20:46.790 --> 00:20:48.690
This is where the metadata becomes really
important.

00:20:48.690 --> 00:20:53.930
But also any APIs that can access the data,
and you also want, particularly if you're

00:20:53.930 --> 00:20:55.810
using the cloud, containers.

00:20:55.810 --> 00:20:59.640
A common form of that would be, say, Docker
containers.

00:20:59.640 --> 00:21:03.410
So there are a series of services that enable
you to use this data in these particular environments,

00:21:03.410 --> 00:21:07.440
and we need to pay attention to those, as
well as the scientific analysis tools that

00:21:07.440 --> 00:21:10.050
you want to use to actually operate on the
data.

00:21:10.050 --> 00:21:14.550
And on the top of all of that, I think what
you want is to have some form of interface

00:21:14.550 --> 00:21:19.300
which allows you to access a collection of
these data sets appropriately, and some sort

00:21:19.300 --> 00:21:25.690
of, really, marketplace to allow those interactions
to drive innovation.

00:21:25.690 --> 00:21:28.750
The other thing you need to do is, obviously,
have digital object compliance, and this touches

00:21:28.750 --> 00:21:30.100
on the FAIR principles.

00:21:30.100 --> 00:21:35.150
So essentially, this stack is three parts--
some form of compute, a whole of data from

00:21:35.150 --> 00:21:40.110
NIH in some form, or biomedical data in general,
and our collections of services and tools

00:21:40.110 --> 00:21:42.670
that allow you to operate on that data-- on
FAIR data.

00:21:42.670 --> 00:21:45.809
If you want more information, there's a link
at the bottom of the slide that takes you

00:21:45.809 --> 00:21:50.779
to far more detailed information at the Commons,
if you want to read about

00:21:50.779 --> 00:21:51.779
[? it.

00:21:51.779 --> 00:21:52.779
?]
A couple of other things I just want to point

00:21:52.779 --> 00:21:56.920
out, again, looking at platforms in general.

00:21:56.920 --> 00:22:00.730
If you look at this in the context of [INAUDIBLE]
the way IT sees it, the bottom layer is really

00:22:00.730 --> 00:22:02.910
the infrastructure as a service.

00:22:02.910 --> 00:22:09.059
And then the layers around the data, and the
tools around those, the services and the analytics,

00:22:09.059 --> 00:22:12.429
these are really platforms for software as
a service.

00:22:12.429 --> 00:22:16.450
And I think this is where we're starting to
see some play, certainly, in the market.

00:22:16.450 --> 00:22:20.500
We're seeing a lot SaaS providers out there,
certainly in biomedical sciences.

00:22:20.500 --> 00:22:24.210
But you're certainly seeing it in other areas
as well, and I'll show a couple of examples

00:22:24.210 --> 00:22:26.090
of those in a minute.

00:22:26.090 --> 00:22:31.270
But I think we're also going to see a drive
to data as a service in the long term, because

00:22:31.270 --> 00:22:33.630
I think, really, data is going to be the currency
to this.

00:22:33.630 --> 00:22:40.440
And how that looks like, I think we're just
starting to understand those pieces.

00:22:40.440 --> 00:22:44.230
Just a moment about digital object compliance,
this is really about making things FAIR.

00:22:44.230 --> 00:22:46.660
I won't go through this in a lot of detail.

00:22:46.660 --> 00:22:50.930
I'll just say that there are key aspects of
this that we need.

00:22:50.930 --> 00:22:56.980
We need digital object identifiers that resolve
to original sources that's machine readable,

00:22:56.980 --> 00:23:02.080
and you have a minimal set of metadata, and
you have clear access rules and entry in indices.

00:23:02.080 --> 00:23:10.700
If you do this, I think what that does is,
it allows us to move towards FAIRness, essentially.

00:23:10.700 --> 00:23:12.340
This slide was done a little while ago.

00:23:12.340 --> 00:23:15.170
So our initial focus was looking at this,
and what we've actually started to do is develop

00:23:15.170 --> 00:23:20.100
working groups within BD2K, but also reaching
out more broadly across the community to look

00:23:20.100 --> 00:23:21.350
at developing FAIR guidelines.

00:23:21.350 --> 00:23:24.190
What does that really mean, to be FAIR?

00:23:24.190 --> 00:23:29.950
And that group, I think, started last year,
and it's just starting to gather steam.

00:23:29.950 --> 00:23:33.200
And the purpose is to develop guidelines to
share those with the community.

00:23:33.200 --> 00:23:37.230
Right, here's some examples of just platforms.

00:23:37.230 --> 00:23:38.320
I'm not endorsing any of these.

00:23:38.320 --> 00:23:39.429
I'm just simply saying, here are examples.

00:23:39.429 --> 00:23:45.290
This is a SaaS application that a lot of people
who do genomics probably know, which is DNAnexus,

00:23:45.290 --> 00:23:51.160
that allows you, essentially, to develop--
to use pipelines and various tools, essentially,

00:23:51.160 --> 00:23:52.640
over clouds.

00:23:52.640 --> 00:23:56.770
And they have a lot of genomic data, and the
purpose of this company, I think, is to enable

00:23:56.770 --> 00:23:58.960
scientists to really use data at scale.

00:23:58.960 --> 00:24:03.160
And it's very much a platform that follows
some of the principles I just showed for the

00:24:03.160 --> 00:24:04.160
Commons.

00:24:04.160 --> 00:24:06.950
Another one is, in this example, is SevenBridges,
as well.

00:24:06.950 --> 00:24:10.210
I think they fall into the same category of
a SaaS.

00:24:10.210 --> 00:24:17.980
And as you'll notice, many of them call themselves
platforms, for good reasons, I believe.

00:24:17.980 --> 00:24:22.360
So I think what you'll find is that the Data
Commons, therefore, drives this digital ecosystem.

00:24:22.360 --> 00:24:26.010
It's the pieces behind it that enable you
to do work with that data.

00:24:26.010 --> 00:24:31.250
And so now, what I want to do is switch a
little bit to talk about some Data Commons

00:24:31.250 --> 00:24:35.830
Pilots and talk about some of the work that
we're doing in NIH.

00:24:35.830 --> 00:24:36.830
I'm not covering all of it.

00:24:36.830 --> 00:24:41.679
I'm just covering some examples of that, that
sort of enable you to hopefully understand

00:24:41.679 --> 00:24:45.870
a little bit more about the concept of the
platform and the Commons.

00:24:45.870 --> 00:24:50.470
So one of the ones we're working on is really
looking at the co-location of large and/or

00:24:50.470 --> 00:24:56.960
highly utilized NIH-funded data, with storage
and compute infrastructure, i.e. a cloud.

00:24:56.960 --> 00:25:01.500
And they're commonly used tools for the analysis
and the sharing of those digital objects.

00:25:01.500 --> 00:25:05.470
The purpose is to create an interoperable
resource for the research community.

00:25:05.470 --> 00:25:09.880
Another key factor is, the purpose of the
cloud isn't just necessarily because the data

00:25:09.880 --> 00:25:10.880
is large.

00:25:10.880 --> 00:25:14.070
It's also a place for collaboration and sharing
of digital objects.

00:25:14.070 --> 00:25:17.850
And I think this is one of the key things
that we've also found, that researchers often

00:25:17.850 --> 00:25:22.010
do their analysis, they use, say, a cloud
simply because they have large data sets,

00:25:22.010 --> 00:25:25.960
but they often want to share or collaborate
as projects are going on.

00:25:25.960 --> 00:25:31.049
And collaborators and investigators are located
geographically in different places across

00:25:31.049 --> 00:25:33.110
the United States or across the world.

00:25:33.110 --> 00:25:36.830
So these environments allow them to do these
kinds of collaborations much more easily,

00:25:36.830 --> 00:25:41.809
and look at the output of the results, collaborate
together.

00:25:41.809 --> 00:25:46.371
Just want to point out that the Commons that
I'm talking about in the Platform-- there

00:25:46.371 --> 00:25:48.140
are the commonses developing out there.

00:25:48.140 --> 00:25:51.410
And I want to point out a couple of these.

00:25:51.410 --> 00:25:56.720
Probably one of the best-known ones is the
Genomic Data Commons from NCI an NIH, which

00:25:56.720 --> 00:26:03.170
is looking at much of the TCGA data loaded
up into that system, then made available exactly

00:26:03.170 --> 00:26:06.610
the same way I've been talking about in the
general commons concept.

00:26:06.610 --> 00:26:10.690
But certainly, in the last year or so, we've
also seen the emergence of other ones.

00:26:10.690 --> 00:26:17.070
So the Fred Hutch Cancer Center over in Seattle,
also, is developing the Hutch Data Commonwealth.

00:26:17.070 --> 00:26:21.260
And the New York Genome Center, under Toby
Bloom, has been developing, also, the New

00:26:21.260 --> 00:26:22.940
York Genome Center Commons.

00:26:22.940 --> 00:26:30.760
There's also been work done at NIH, at NAID,
the Allergy and Infectious Diseases Institute,

00:26:30.760 --> 00:26:36.190
developing, essentially, a microbiome platform,
as well, for analysis.

00:26:36.190 --> 00:26:39.190
So what we're seeing is a collection of commonses
happening.

00:26:39.190 --> 00:26:43.330
The structure of them is very similar to what
I've just described before, is the platform

00:26:43.330 --> 00:26:44.330
stack.

00:26:44.330 --> 00:26:49.090
They're all using the basic premise, but the
data is somewhat different, depending on what

00:26:49.090 --> 00:26:51.470
the particular data sets they are.

00:26:51.470 --> 00:26:55.510
But I think it's important to see that they
all have fundamentally the same principles,

00:26:55.510 --> 00:26:57.049
and they're all starting to come together.

00:26:57.049 --> 00:26:59.190
And one of the other things is, how do we
connect to them?

00:26:59.190 --> 00:27:03.360
And I'll get to that in a minute.

00:27:03.360 --> 00:27:05.830
So I'm going to talk a little bit, now, about
the Pilot.

00:27:05.830 --> 00:27:06.830
But I just want to emphasize that this is
just an

00:27:06.830 --> 00:27:07.830
So I'm going to talk a little bit, now, about
the Pilot.

00:27:07.830 --> 00:27:08.830
But I just want to emphasize that this is
just an example.

00:27:08.830 --> 00:27:11.460
The data sets I'm going to show here are not
the ones we're going to use.

00:27:11.460 --> 00:27:14.230
They're just examples of the kinds of things
that we have.

00:27:14.230 --> 00:27:19.230
So it's just a draft, but we are actually
working on the principles behind this.

00:27:19.230 --> 00:27:24.640
And some time early this year, in the first
quarter, we'll be able to announce some of

00:27:24.640 --> 00:27:29.770
the work that we're actually doing in this
area, and some of the specifics around it.

00:27:29.770 --> 00:27:35.580
So I'm going to take the platform and just
give an example around some of this.

00:27:35.580 --> 00:27:40.530
So I think what we've got here is, we have
a platform.

00:27:40.530 --> 00:27:43.860
So we have a collection of different clouds
that we can use.

00:27:43.860 --> 00:27:48.440
I think it's important to look at a variety
of different clouds, not just one, in part

00:27:48.440 --> 00:27:52.110
because we obviously want to avoid some level
of vendor lock-in.

00:27:52.110 --> 00:27:59.230
But I think it also is helpful for supporting
innovation and competition amongst them.

00:27:59.230 --> 00:28:00.670
And each one of the systems is different.

00:28:00.670 --> 00:28:02.720
They do offer slightly different services.

00:28:02.720 --> 00:28:05.790
That's potentially a problem, and also an
opportunity.

00:28:05.790 --> 00:28:09.480
So I think we're looking at a collection of
those, which ones we choose is going to be

00:28:09.480 --> 00:28:13.860
dependent upon some of the things that we
do based on our use cases.

00:28:13.860 --> 00:28:17.380
The other thing we're doing there is looking
at-- if I'm taking this platform, [INAUDIBLE]

00:28:17.380 --> 00:28:19.860
we would take a collection of data.

00:28:19.860 --> 00:28:24.330
So here are just some examples of data sets,
which are supported by NIH.

00:28:24.330 --> 00:28:26.820
I'm not saying that these would be the ultimate
ones we use.

00:28:26.820 --> 00:28:30.780
But essentially, what you're creating is a
collection of data.

00:28:30.780 --> 00:28:34.620
They're different types, they don't necessarily
interoperate with each other.

00:28:34.620 --> 00:28:40.169
But essentially, you're creating a pool of
data that people could potentially query individually

00:28:40.169 --> 00:28:43.380
or across, and I think that's pretty important.

00:28:43.380 --> 00:28:47.250
And from that is going to come from the need
to potentially harmonize, or not, and that's

00:28:47.250 --> 00:28:48.440
a question that we need to look at.

00:28:48.440 --> 00:28:53.480
But I think a pool of data would actually
be quite useful to consider.

00:28:53.480 --> 00:29:00.350
So if we take that a little bit further, then
we could layer on top of that a series of

00:29:00.350 --> 00:29:02.100
analytics and services.

00:29:02.100 --> 00:29:04.220
And these are just examples of some of those.

00:29:04.220 --> 00:29:07.110
Some of those are commercial, some of them
are not commercial.

00:29:07.110 --> 00:29:12.250
Some of them are tools, some of them are tool
systems, some of them are platforms as a service,

00:29:12.250 --> 00:29:17.130
and the Global Alliance is obviously looking
at some standard APIs for querying the data.

00:29:17.130 --> 00:29:21.150
So this is a collection of things that already
exist out there, that could essentially layer

00:29:21.150 --> 00:29:26.400
over the top of this, that could leverage
the data in a variety of different forms and

00:29:26.400 --> 00:29:32.790
provide access to the rest of the biomedical
community.

00:29:32.790 --> 00:29:35.669
The other thing that I think you want to look
at is, you need to think, obviously, about

00:29:35.669 --> 00:29:36.669
the indexing.

00:29:36.669 --> 00:29:42.240
And there's work being done inside BD2K with
bioCADDIE, which is an indexing system, and

00:29:42.240 --> 00:29:47.440
Bioschemas, which has been discussed with
[? Alexia, ?] which is the group in Europe,

00:29:47.440 --> 00:29:49.640
looking at schema [INAUDIBLE] applications
to biomedical sciences.

00:29:49.640 --> 00:29:53.930
And I know bioCADDIE has been heavily involved
in looking at those, as well, and incorporating

00:29:53.930 --> 00:29:56.140
a lot of that work within DataMed.

00:29:56.140 --> 00:30:00.360
But the key point I'm trying to make here
is that you need some form indexing.

00:30:00.360 --> 00:30:04.650
I'm pointing out these ones here are just
as examples, in part because we're having

00:30:04.650 --> 00:30:09.800
a BD2K lecture, and we are planning to leverage
the work that bioCADDIE [? hands.

00:30:09.800 --> 00:30:13.630
?] But it's really critical that you actually
consider the indexing of that.

00:30:13.630 --> 00:30:16.730
Otherwise, what you have is a whole lot of
data and a whole lot of tools that are not

00:30:16.730 --> 00:30:18.210
necessarily easily findable.

00:30:18.210 --> 00:30:23.870
And I think that's really important, to actually
have here and call that in the system.

00:30:23.870 --> 00:30:31.990
I think another aspect here is how we would
leverage this, potentially, at NIH.

00:30:31.990 --> 00:30:37.660
And I think a real key factor there is, the
things that we can actually do is, if we make

00:30:37.660 --> 00:30:42.350
this data available-- these large data sets
available in the cloud, and we can layer on

00:30:42.350 --> 00:30:48.370
these tools, I think there are multiple ways
that NIH can foster the innovation of further

00:30:48.370 --> 00:30:50.210
development in this area.

00:30:50.210 --> 00:30:54.730
The tools that I've laid out here in this--
around the analytics and services are currently

00:30:54.730 --> 00:30:55.800
tools that have been developed.

00:30:55.800 --> 00:30:57.990
But I think we want to foster more of that.

00:30:57.990 --> 00:31:02.410
And I've just given some examples here of--
[? our ones ?] were a bread-and-butter grant

00:31:02.410 --> 00:31:04.240
from NIH, but there are other ways to do this.

00:31:04.240 --> 00:31:08.650
Essentially, there are ways to get grants
at NIH to foster innovation over the use of

00:31:08.650 --> 00:31:09.650
this particular data.

00:31:09.650 --> 00:31:12.330
And I think we definitely want to do that.

00:31:12.330 --> 00:31:16.030
But I think what we also want to look at is
consider looking challenges and prizes.

00:31:16.030 --> 00:31:17.150
Why?

00:31:17.150 --> 00:31:20.650
Because I think it could be really interesting
to sort of ask questions across some of this

00:31:20.650 --> 00:31:25.850
data, assuming the consents allow us to do
that, to really kind of think about new ways

00:31:25.850 --> 00:31:29.941
that you can actually intersect and ask questions
across that data to enable discovery in a

00:31:29.941 --> 00:31:31.320
different way.

00:31:31.320 --> 00:31:33.630
You can do that, I think, from a couple of
different angles.

00:31:33.630 --> 00:31:37.700
One, from a biomedical one, where you can
ask an interesting biomedical question across

00:31:37.700 --> 00:31:43.730
these biomedical data sets to improve the
outcome-- the biomarker, some sort of new

00:31:43.730 --> 00:31:47.740
discovery, a better understanding of a disease,
all of those are relevant.

00:31:47.740 --> 00:31:51.870
But I think what is also relevant is the services
and tools that are needed to do that at a

00:31:51.870 --> 00:31:54.200
faster and more accurate rate.

00:31:54.200 --> 00:31:56.360
So how well can you predict a biomarker?

00:31:56.360 --> 00:31:58.299
What's the best way you can actually do that?

00:31:58.299 --> 00:32:00.680
What are the tools that you need to do that?

00:32:00.680 --> 00:32:01.980
What's the statistics you need to do that?

00:32:01.980 --> 00:32:05.110
Those are some of the aspects, I think, here,
that some of these challenges and prizes could

00:32:05.110 --> 00:32:09.730
also address, that I think could yield to
some pretty interesting ways that we could

00:32:09.730 --> 00:32:13.030
leverage this particular data.

00:32:13.030 --> 00:32:20.490
I think the other thing I want to point out
is that over the top of this whole thing,

00:32:20.490 --> 00:32:24.150
you want to have this marketplace, which is--
I'm using the term app store, because that's

00:32:24.150 --> 00:32:28.780
where people really have their best understanding
of how it works, when you use an app, and

00:32:28.780 --> 00:32:32.710
it works over the top of data, or within your
system.

00:32:32.710 --> 00:32:35.789
But I think, really, I'm going towards the
concept of a marketplace.

00:32:35.789 --> 00:32:41.100
How do we actually have a collection of tools,
systems, data that you can easily find and

00:32:41.100 --> 00:32:46.150
create a marketplace for the way that you
operate over particular data sets or particular

00:32:46.150 --> 00:32:47.150
tools?

00:32:47.150 --> 00:32:52.390
What's the right way to think about that,
rather than a single way to view a data through

00:32:52.390 --> 00:32:53.830
one set of tools alone?

00:32:53.830 --> 00:32:57.080
I think that's pretty critical.

00:32:57.080 --> 00:33:01.130
Another aspect to consider about here is what
I call the authorization authentication layer,

00:33:01.130 --> 00:33:08.180
and there is the-- a lot of this data was
likely to have human subjects issues.

00:33:08.180 --> 00:33:10.340
So some will not, but some does.

00:33:10.340 --> 00:33:15.660
So for example, human microbiome data does
not, but many of the other data sets do.

00:33:15.660 --> 00:33:21.510
So we obviously need to think of the right
way to handle that from NIH.

00:33:21.510 --> 00:33:26.810
And much of the data that's human based is
stored at dbGaP at NCBI.

00:33:26.810 --> 00:33:32.309
So there's a critical discussion, there, to
happen about, how do you set up the authorization

00:33:32.309 --> 00:33:37.610
authentication layer that allows you to get
access to that data in the appropriate way?

00:33:37.610 --> 00:33:41.340
And we've been having conversations around
that, within NIH, about how that might actually

00:33:41.340 --> 00:33:42.340
occur.

00:33:42.340 --> 00:33:46.220
But that's pretty critical, so that you can
ensure that only the people that are permitted

00:33:46.220 --> 00:33:49.920
to use the data can actually access that.

00:33:49.920 --> 00:33:53.370
Another thing that I want to point out here
is that this isn't the only Commons.

00:33:53.370 --> 00:33:55.539
What we're looking at here is NIH-funded data.

00:33:55.539 --> 00:34:03.020
But as I pointed out before, there's a collection
of different commonses that are being developed.

00:34:03.020 --> 00:34:08.700
And I think what's really important, there,
is that we start talking with them now, not

00:34:08.700 --> 00:34:09.700
at the end.

00:34:09.700 --> 00:34:13.109
Otherwise, we create cylinders of excellence,
and that's not what we want.

00:34:13.109 --> 00:34:17.280
So I've certainly had conversations with all
of the folks that I pointed out before, and

00:34:17.280 --> 00:34:20.530
there are more and more folks thinking about
this.

00:34:20.530 --> 00:34:26.320
And we've started some discussions around,
what's the right way for these pieces to interact

00:34:26.320 --> 00:34:27.320
and intersect?

00:34:27.320 --> 00:34:28.320
And where are they separate?

00:34:28.320 --> 00:34:30.659
And they need to be separate.

00:34:30.659 --> 00:34:35.940
So obviously, one of the most obvious ones
is to look at it through an API access point

00:34:35.940 --> 00:34:38.109
between these commonses.

00:34:38.109 --> 00:34:42.340
And looking at how we can run services between
them, it's complicated, especially when we

00:34:42.340 --> 00:34:43.640
deal with human subjects' data.

00:34:43.640 --> 00:34:48.119
But I think the conversation is the important
point here-- that we don't want to simply

00:34:48.119 --> 00:34:51.770
look at providing data and creating innovation
on the topic

00:34:51.770 --> 00:34:52.770
point here-- that we don't want to simply
look at providing data and creating innovation

00:34:52.770 --> 00:34:55.599
on the topic from NIH, and not connecting
to what other folks are already doing in their

00:34:55.599 --> 00:34:59.830
other commonses.

00:34:59.830 --> 00:35:07.119
So I have some considerations I want to just
talk through now, and that is metrics.

00:35:07.119 --> 00:35:11.520
What I'm describing here is essentially a
platform to support biomedical data that uses

00:35:11.520 --> 00:35:14.660
cloud data and services.

00:35:14.660 --> 00:35:19.670
But understanding how, and you account for
all of the data usage, is really important,

00:35:19.670 --> 00:35:23.940
because there will be-- we need to understand
how it's being used and why.

00:35:23.940 --> 00:35:27.690
There's also a cost, particularly for these
large data sets.

00:35:27.690 --> 00:35:31.160
Cloud storage is not cheap for the large data
sets, particularly if you want to put them

00:35:31.160 --> 00:35:32.400
in more than one cloud.

00:35:32.400 --> 00:35:37.359
In saying that, many of the commercial providers
are very interested in working with us, because

00:35:37.359 --> 00:35:43.000
they see the value of a platform and an ecosystem
that is more than just data storage.

00:35:43.000 --> 00:35:48.910
So we are in, obviously, lots of deep conversations
with them about the right way to interact.

00:35:48.910 --> 00:35:52.540
This is also a pay-for-use cloud compute.

00:35:52.540 --> 00:35:56.210
There is a project which is being developed
in NIH called the NIH credits model, and I'm

00:35:56.210 --> 00:35:58.850
not going to speak about that today.

00:35:58.850 --> 00:36:03.590
But it's really looking at, how do you pay
for those services directly, and how does

00:36:03.590 --> 00:36:06.690
NIH really understand what people are paying
for?

00:36:06.690 --> 00:36:12.420
For example, when you are using cloud services,
a lot of the time, you get free credits from

00:36:12.420 --> 00:36:15.750
the cloud providers per se, which is great.

00:36:15.750 --> 00:36:19.990
But if you probably tried to include everything
that you used on the cloud in your NIH grant,

00:36:19.990 --> 00:36:23.460
it probably would be very expensive, and it
would take a fairly large chunk out of your

00:36:23.460 --> 00:36:24.460
grant.

00:36:24.460 --> 00:36:28.950
There are also other issues that we need to
deal with, which is the indirect costs for

00:36:28.950 --> 00:36:29.950
cloud.

00:36:29.950 --> 00:36:33.099
When you submit a grant to NIH, there are
indirect costs, which are associated with

00:36:33.099 --> 00:36:34.510
the research institution.

00:36:34.510 --> 00:36:39.349
And I think what we need to look at is the
way that these things, obviously, interact,

00:36:39.349 --> 00:36:42.170
and what's the right way to pay for these
and also to deal with the institutions?

00:36:42.170 --> 00:36:49.080
And now, let's be clear-- the commercial clouds
are useful in the sense that they have scale,

00:36:49.080 --> 00:36:54.540
but there are many groups out there that have
already developed private or institutional-based

00:36:54.540 --> 00:36:55.940
clouds.

00:36:55.940 --> 00:36:57.430
And there's good reason for doing that.

00:36:57.430 --> 00:37:01.950
Cost is one, privacy of data-- there's been
a lot of concern, if you put this commercial

00:37:01.950 --> 00:37:04.100
clouds, what's the right way of handling that?

00:37:04.100 --> 00:37:06.900
I think we're still in the midst in determining
those things.

00:37:06.900 --> 00:37:12.850
And so I think we will see situations where
hybrid clouds really are important and necessary.

00:37:12.850 --> 00:37:17.010
And we need to look at what's the right mix
between those.

00:37:17.010 --> 00:37:20.359
And that's pretty-- a very important thing
to look at.

00:37:20.359 --> 00:37:24.910
We have to look at managing open- versus controlled-access
data.

00:37:24.910 --> 00:37:28.570
The controlled-access data is
obviously related to human.

00:37:28.570 --> 00:37:31.300
And we've got to look at, what's the right
way to manage that?

00:37:31.300 --> 00:37:36.440
I mentioned that before, that the authorization,
potentially through a single sign-on, is the

00:37:36.440 --> 00:37:37.440
way to do that.

00:37:37.440 --> 00:37:40.581
But is that really a dream or a nightmare,
because it's-- from what we've seen, it's

00:37:40.581 --> 00:37:44.740
really a very complicated set of things to
do.

00:37:44.740 --> 00:37:48.810
And there may be reasons where a single sign-on
is good, but there may be situations where

00:37:48.810 --> 00:37:52.640
that's not possible, so we need to sort of
test all of that and figure out what's the

00:37:52.640 --> 00:37:55.730
right way to approach it.

00:37:55.730 --> 00:38:00.070
It's important to point out that we need to
look at the difference between archiving versus

00:38:00.070 --> 00:38:03.380
working and versioning copies of the data.

00:38:03.380 --> 00:38:09.460
NIH requires that we keep all our data, certainly
the-- we archive it.

00:38:09.460 --> 00:38:17.480
That's where very large archives like NCBI
are very important in EBI over in Europe.

00:38:17.480 --> 00:38:21.599
But if we put working copies of the data onto
the cloud, they're not archives, and we need

00:38:21.599 --> 00:38:23.810
to know that that's what we're actually doing
and why.

00:38:23.810 --> 00:38:28.780
Another one is, if we do move copies of large
data sets to the cloud, obviously, versioning

00:38:28.780 --> 00:38:31.119
is going to be pretty important.

00:38:31.119 --> 00:38:33.430
So how do we handle that?

00:38:33.430 --> 00:38:39.540
That leads to all sorts of issues around syncing,
and I think whatever we do in testing this,

00:38:39.540 --> 00:38:42.359
we need to figure that out.

00:38:42.359 --> 00:38:46.150
Another one, which I mentioned before, is
interoperability with other commonses.

00:38:46.150 --> 00:38:47.599
Many of them are going to be based on clouds.

00:38:47.599 --> 00:38:50.760
The ones I mentioned to you before are going
to be based on clouds.

00:38:50.760 --> 00:38:57.410
And it would be important to ensure that we
don't have these cylinders of excellence,

00:38:57.410 --> 00:38:59.780
that we actually have interoperability between
these clouds.

00:38:59.780 --> 00:39:06.000
We also have standards around metadata, UIDs,
and the APIs.

00:39:06.000 --> 00:39:11.700
And the key, I think, here is to reuse what
we've already-- available to us in the community.

00:39:11.700 --> 00:39:16.670
There is work being done, certainly within
BD2K around metadata templates by Mark Musen's

00:39:16.670 --> 00:39:19.200
group at CEDAR.

00:39:19.200 --> 00:39:24.310
There's a lot of work being done by various
groups with UIDs, and there's the Global Alliance,

00:39:24.310 --> 00:39:26.660
which has been looking at open-standard APIs.

00:39:26.660 --> 00:39:34.040
There's also work being done within some commons,
smaller efforts looking at open-standard APIs

00:39:34.040 --> 00:39:38.320
that incorporate metadata, and that are smart,
and actually know they can find different

00:39:38.320 --> 00:39:43.550
APIs and locate them, so that we have a better
understanding of what's available to us.

00:39:43.550 --> 00:39:49.890
But this is critically an important area that
we need to have further development in.

00:39:49.890 --> 00:39:51.420
Discoverability touches on the indexing issue.

00:39:51.420 --> 00:39:56.530
I mentioned bioCADDIE before, but we need
to look at-- bioCADDIE's one place to start.

00:39:56.530 --> 00:40:00.580
And it's a really good place to have-- to
use.

00:40:00.580 --> 00:40:03.540
But there are other ways to do that, and we
need to-- and the field is changing, so we

00:40:03.540 --> 00:40:07.280
need to figure out what's the right way to
support that kind of work.

00:40:07.280 --> 00:40:08.730
And particularly, if we're going to have data
and digital

00:40:08.730 --> 00:40:09.730
the right way to support that kind of work.

00:40:09.730 --> 00:40:11.510
And particularly, if we're going to have data
and digital objects of various sorts across

00:40:11.510 --> 00:40:16.670
different clouds, that's going to be an interesting
problem to try and solve, as well.

00:40:16.670 --> 00:40:21.540
Regarding interfaces, I think that's really
pretty critical, because you've got users

00:40:21.540 --> 00:40:23.609
with different needs and different capabilities.

00:40:23.609 --> 00:40:28.731
Certainly, in the early versions of what we're
trying to do here, will probably the most

00:40:28.731 --> 00:40:34.890
usable by those that have a whole lot of bioinformatics
capabilities already, and understanding.

00:40:34.890 --> 00:40:41.310
But we understand very clearly that biomedical
researchers don't necessarily have the capacity,

00:40:41.310 --> 00:40:44.700
all the time, to have bioinformaticians in
their group.

00:40:44.700 --> 00:40:48.560
And so we need to be able to have interfaces
that are available to biologists that don't

00:40:48.560 --> 00:40:52.580
have those capabilities, but definitely want
to be able to use the data.

00:40:52.580 --> 00:40:57.760
So that's something we're really being very
mindful of in all of these pieces.

00:40:57.760 --> 00:40:59.560
Consent-- this is a really important piece.

00:40:59.560 --> 00:41:03.420
So we're getting onto some policy pieces here.

00:41:03.420 --> 00:41:07.349
Reconsenting of the data may be necessary,
and that also could be very complex, particularly

00:41:07.349 --> 00:41:11.310
if you're trying to traverse across two different
data sets that weren't consented to be using

00:41:11.310 --> 00:41:16.220
for the purpose of using them to mine across
two data sets.

00:41:16.220 --> 00:41:20.460
So we have to be very mindful of those consents
in the first place.

00:41:20.460 --> 00:41:22.980
And so engaging with the right folks to talk
about that is really important.

00:41:22.980 --> 00:41:25.090
We've certainly been doing that at NIH.

00:41:25.090 --> 00:41:32.890
There are policies that need to be also considered
in such an endeavor, and that is the data

00:41:32.890 --> 00:41:38.109
sharing policies that are useful and effective
to support this kind of open science.

00:41:38.109 --> 00:41:41.720
We have some already at NIH, but I certainly
think we need a further focus on this, and

00:41:41.720 --> 00:41:46.540
there are certainly efforts underway at NIH
to do this.

00:41:46.540 --> 00:41:50.010
And I think we need more effort in this area,
and I think NIH understands that.

00:41:50.010 --> 00:41:52.750
I think we also need to keep pace with the
use of technology.

00:41:52.750 --> 00:41:58.359
And here, the example I'd give you is the
example of dbGaP data in the cloud.

00:41:58.359 --> 00:42:04.520
This is-- a couple of years ago, the policy
precluded the use of dbGaP data in the cloud.

00:42:04.520 --> 00:42:08.430
This was a big problem for researchers, since
they wanted to use that data in the cloud,

00:42:08.430 --> 00:42:12.610
because they had-- it was large, and they
also had large data that they wanted to compare

00:42:12.610 --> 00:42:13.610
it against.

00:42:13.610 --> 00:42:15.450
But policies precluded the use of that.

00:42:15.450 --> 00:42:20.599
And so a group of us, including myself, helped
change some of the policies around that to

00:42:20.599 --> 00:42:22.790
enable the use of dbGaP data in the cloud.

00:42:22.790 --> 00:42:26.490
So sometimes what happens is, technology is
further ahead than the policies.

00:42:26.490 --> 00:42:31.089
And we need to do a good job of making sure
that these two things are aligned.

00:42:31.089 --> 00:42:35.530
And again, it means that we talk-- in the
case, the Office of Science Policy, and work

00:42:35.530 --> 00:42:39.190
with those people to ensure that they understand
what we're trying to do,

00:42:39.190 --> 00:42:44.040
and vice versa, so we can keep up to speed
with the changes in the technologies.

00:42:44.040 --> 00:42:50.300
I think incentives are incredibly important
here for the shareability of FAIR data, as

00:42:50.300 --> 00:42:53.550
part of the grant review criteria of NIH.

00:42:53.550 --> 00:42:59.050
By that I mean, when you submit an application
to NIH, you obviously missed all of your papers,

00:42:59.050 --> 00:43:04.770
but it would be really good if people could
also cite their data and software, and any

00:43:04.770 --> 00:43:10.320
of the other data assets-- digital assets
that you have in a way that's meaningful and

00:43:10.320 --> 00:43:11.320
consistent.

00:43:11.320 --> 00:43:14.700
So I think we don't have any incentives in
there right now, but I think we're really

00:43:14.700 --> 00:43:19.320
looking at the fact that if data becomes currency,
and FAIR data becomes currency, what's the

00:43:19.320 --> 00:43:25.730
right way to include that in the review system
when you're putting in applications to NIH?

00:43:25.730 --> 00:43:31.100
I think we also need to look at governance,
which is, how do we do this?

00:43:31.100 --> 00:43:36.160
How do we actually govern the overall way
that we handle a commons?

00:43:36.160 --> 00:43:40.570
I think what's critical here is to have community
involvement in this governance model that

00:43:40.570 --> 00:43:42.940
isn't just generated by NIH.

00:43:42.940 --> 00:43:47.200
If this is really going to be an open-- a
platform for open science, and an ecosystem

00:43:47.200 --> 00:43:52.160
to be able to use data at scale and to share
it, then I think getting an understanding

00:43:52.160 --> 00:43:55.530
from community involvement is really important.

00:43:55.530 --> 00:44:00.010
And I think a key one here, the last one I've
got here on my slides, is sustainability.

00:44:00.010 --> 00:44:04.140
We all know that grants have a cycle at NIH,
and then they end.

00:44:04.140 --> 00:44:06.440
So what's the long-term support for this?

00:44:06.440 --> 00:44:10.850
If we do move data, for example, in the cloud
for some of these large data sets, how do

00:44:10.850 --> 00:44:12.980
we sustain that in the longer term?

00:44:12.980 --> 00:44:16.910
What's the right business model to do that,
and what's the right way to attempt to do

00:44:16.910 --> 00:44:17.910
that?

00:44:17.910 --> 00:44:22.330
I think these are all questions that we're
having in terms of what we are trying to think

00:44:22.330 --> 00:44:24.720
about, how we approach the commons.

00:44:24.720 --> 00:44:30.420
So I think I've got just to my last slide
here, which is the acknowledgements.

00:44:30.420 --> 00:44:33.589
And there's a lot of people that have been
involved in this.

00:44:33.589 --> 00:44:37.770
And most-- there will be some folks who recognize
the names here.

00:44:37.770 --> 00:44:42.550
But my point I want to make out is that there's
a lot of work happening across NIH that isn't

00:44:42.550 --> 00:44:44.210
just within one Institute.

00:44:44.210 --> 00:44:50.609
It's across many institutes and in places
that are unusual, so to speak, and sometimes

00:44:50.609 --> 00:44:51.839
we do bio work.

00:44:51.839 --> 00:44:57.539
And that is CIT, the Center for Information
Technology, and Andrea Norris's group, who's

00:44:57.539 --> 00:45:02.109
also the CIO of NIH, which is all the pure
computing side has been working heavily with

00:45:02.109 --> 00:45:03.109
us.

00:45:03.109 --> 00:45:07.800
Also, Common Fund, who's very interested in
this kind of work, has been working with us.

00:45:07.800 --> 00:45:13.410
I've listed, also, the Clinical Center is
working with us-- the NIH Clinical Center--

00:45:13.410 --> 00:45:16.250
to look at some of their data.

00:45:16.250 --> 00:45:18.330
And also, the Office of Science Policy.

00:45:18.330 --> 00:45:20.060
So it's a collection of folks across NIH.

00:45:20.060 --> 00:45:23.460
But I'd also like to just have a shout-out
to Research and Industry.

00:45:23.460 --> 00:45:27.920
We've spent a lot of time
working with our industry, primarily cloud

00:45:27.920 --> 00:45:33.900
providers, but also the SaaS providers, and
also other folks in the community developing

00:45:33.900 --> 00:45:35.000
commonses.

00:45:35.000 --> 00:45:38.230
And they have been providing really good and
helpful input into the way we're thinking

00:45:38.230 --> 00:45:40.099
about the commons.

00:45:40.099 --> 00:45:44.910
So it's really a unified effort across NIH
in these discussions, in places not just across

00:45:44.910 --> 00:45:50.230
certain institutes and centers, but in places
that wouldn't normally be involved in this.

00:45:50.230 --> 00:45:54.240
So it's really touching on the issue that
data is really the currency of the way we

00:45:54.240 --> 00:45:56.990
have to go forward.

00:45:56.990 --> 00:46:00.070
So I'm just going to end by just saying thank
you for your time.

00:46:00.070 --> 00:46:04.660
I hope that this has helped you understand
a little bit about the path that we took to

00:46:04.660 --> 00:46:09.061
the Data Commons, that we have some idea of
some Pilots we want to do for these large

00:46:09.061 --> 00:46:10.589
data sets.

00:46:10.589 --> 00:46:15.200
And in the coming months, we'll be able to
explain a lot more detail around what that

00:46:15.200 --> 00:46:16.200
will be.

00:46:16.200 --> 00:46:21.260
But essentially, what NIH would like to do
is to enable the use and access to NIH data

00:46:21.260 --> 00:46:23.210
sets to the community.

00:46:23.210 --> 00:46:26.910
And the innovation we hope will come from
the community, and how you will use those

00:46:26.910 --> 00:46:32.280
data, and how you will actually develop tools,
systems, services, platforms, et cetera, over

00:46:32.280 --> 00:46:34.230
the top of that.

00:46:34.230 --> 00:46:36.490
The data sets are large, and they cost.

00:46:36.490 --> 00:46:40.020
I think this is where NIH really needs to
play in the game, and we need to help the

00:46:40.020 --> 00:46:46.740
community get access to that data so that
we can innovate both in the data science realm,

00:46:46.740 --> 00:46:50.520
as well as the biomedical realm, so that we
can actually foster new discoveries.

00:46:50.520 --> 00:46:54.470
All right, I think that's where I'm going
to stop right now.

00:46:54.470 --> 00:46:57.550
And I think we're going to take some questions,
is that right?

00:46:57.550 --> 00:46:58.550
It is.

00:46:58.550 --> 00:47:04.550
Thank you, Vivien, for this great overview,
and for giving us an update on what's going

00:47:04.550 --> 00:47:05.550
on with the NIH data commons.

00:47:05.550 --> 00:47:07.830
So we've actually got quite a few questions
for you.

00:47:07.830 --> 00:47:08.840
I'll just go through some of them.

00:47:08.840 --> 00:47:15.190
We'll start with this first one, which sort
of has to do with the fees that are always

00:47:15.190 --> 00:47:16.190
associated with big data.

00:47:16.190 --> 00:47:19.670
And this is actually touching on the veracity
component.

00:47:19.670 --> 00:47:25.930
And the question is really, given that we're
moving toward these sort of open data repositories,

00:47:25.930 --> 00:47:30.840
and public access to this information, and
obviously, sharing the information, as well,

00:47:30.840 --> 00:47:36.849
and people being able to put their own data
into it, how do you know that you can trust

00:47:36.849 --> 00:47:37.849
the data?

00:47:37.849 --> 00:47:41.369
How do we actually get to veracity of data?

00:47:41.369 --> 00:47:46.290
Right, and I think that comes down to some
of the FAIR guidelines, and making sure that

00:47:46.290 --> 00:47:52.050
you know that when people apply them, what's
the right way that we can-- I don't want to

00:47:52.050 --> 00:47:54.920
use the term policing, but we need to know
that we can do that.

00:47:54.920 --> 00:47:58.130
And that's a complex issue.

00:47:58.130 --> 00:48:01.770
I think if we can
agree to a set of guidelines of what FAIRness

00:48:01.770 --> 00:48:09.180
is, and then we can empower ourselves to use
those guidelines, I think that's important.

00:48:09.180 --> 00:48:13.920
I think another thing that we need to consider
is that, as people start using them, if more

00:48:13.920 --> 00:48:20.150
transactions happen on quality FAIR data,
if they meet the guidelines, then the incentives

00:48:20.150 --> 00:48:21.150
kick into play, right?

00:48:21.150 --> 00:48:26.720
So if more people are using the data, and
it's in FAIR format, and it's compliant, and

00:48:26.720 --> 00:48:31.290
you can quote it, say, for example, in an
application for NIH, that's a good thing.

00:48:31.290 --> 00:48:37.380
So are there are ways to do that where it's
actually driven, in part, by policing policy-making.

00:48:37.380 --> 00:48:42.440
And some of it is, I think, pushed by the
community, where if we agree to a standard,

00:48:42.440 --> 00:48:47.980
it makes the transactions and the systems
stronger, and the data more usable and more

00:48:47.980 --> 00:48:48.980
quotable.

00:48:48.980 --> 00:48:53.230
So I think it comes from a combination of
two different things.

00:48:53.230 --> 00:48:57.359
And I think it also comes from the fact that
we don't yet know how to do all of that.

00:48:57.359 --> 00:49:00.930
But knowing the fact that the data can be
dirty, and not quality, I think is really

00:49:00.930 --> 00:49:02.690
important, too.

00:49:02.690 --> 00:49:07.339
So I think it allows for a little bit of wiggle
room to say, I don't think we know, yet, the

00:49:07.339 --> 00:49:08.730
entirety of the system.

00:49:08.730 --> 00:49:15.560
OK, I mean, it-- definitely, it is an open
question.

00:49:15.560 --> 00:49:17.210
And it's how do we look at that?

00:49:17.210 --> 00:49:26.880
So following up on that sort of question is
another one from-- regarding the culture of

00:49:26.880 --> 00:49:27.880
data sharing.

00:49:27.880 --> 00:49:34.730
And what is your perception of where the community
is in terms of this new culture of data sharing?

00:49:34.730 --> 00:49:40.070
Do you feel that there is a broad acceptance,
or a growing acceptance?

00:49:40.070 --> 00:49:48.170
What proportion of scientists and researchers
do you feel are embracing the data sharing

00:49:48.170 --> 00:49:53.880
and participating in it, versus those that
are basically going to have to be mandated

00:49:53.880 --> 00:49:56.329
to do the data sharing?

00:49:56.329 --> 00:50:02.740
Well, this will be my entire opinion, but
I can try and address it from couple of different

00:50:02.740 --> 00:50:03.740
regions.

00:50:03.740 --> 00:50:07.970
My own personal view is that the participants
come from a couple of different regions.

00:50:07.970 --> 00:50:10.640
I certainly notice it with the younger generation.

00:50:10.640 --> 00:50:17.490
So the postdocs and the grad students-- their
openness, their approach, they come from open.

00:50:17.490 --> 00:50:18.609
So their mindset is open.

00:50:18.609 --> 00:50:20.960
So they tend to share.

00:50:20.960 --> 00:50:29.460
I think as you go up the seniority stack,
so to speak, some of the PIs who are there

00:50:29.460 --> 00:50:33.250
now, who come from an older generation, don't
have that kind of mindset.

00:50:33.250 --> 00:50:35.500
So that creates some issues.

00:50:35.500 --> 00:50:37.690
But that's not true of all of them.

00:50:37.690 --> 00:50:39.790
I think some certainly see the value of it.

00:50:39.790 --> 00:50:43.700
But the mentality, I think, has been changing
in the current generations and the ones that

00:50:43.700 --> 00:50:44.740
are coming through now.

00:50:44.740 --> 00:50:47.080
So that's one of the things that I see.

00:50:47.080 --> 00:50:51.470
And so as the grad students are going through
the system, and becoming professors within

00:50:51.470 --> 00:50:54.000
departments, they'll bring their culture with
them.

00:50:54.000 --> 00:50:57.260
It will take time, but I think we're going
to see that.

00:50:57.260 --> 00:51:01.780
And also, I think we've got some, perhaps,
enlightened souls who are pretty senior in

00:51:01.780 --> 00:51:04.230
their
organizations, who are also beacons of trying

00:51:04.230 --> 00:51:05.230
to push this through.

00:51:05.230 --> 00:51:07.800
And I think there are plenty of examples that
we see.

00:51:07.800 --> 00:51:11.690
So the combination of those, I think, are
what I see changing.

00:51:11.690 --> 00:51:16.720
I think the funding institutions play a crucial
role in this.

00:51:16.720 --> 00:51:20.140
And that is that the funding institutions
also have to provide incentives.

00:51:20.140 --> 00:51:25.421
So as I mentioned during my talk, you need
to think about-- well, we at NIH, need to

00:51:25.421 --> 00:51:29.440
think about the way that this enters the review
system.

00:51:29.440 --> 00:51:35.100
So if there is an incentive for a digital
asset that has either-- primarily, data or

00:51:35.100 --> 00:51:40.230
software is where the talk is right now--
that has, for example, a DOI associated with

00:51:40.230 --> 00:51:46.450
it, that that can actually be listed as something
very important in your grant application.

00:51:46.450 --> 00:51:50.470
And that that has value and weight within
the way that NIH sees it.

00:51:50.470 --> 00:51:51.869
And when it does, a review.

00:51:51.869 --> 00:51:53.010
It's not just about your paper.

00:51:53.010 --> 00:51:55.950
It's all about the other things that have
occurred, as well.

00:51:55.950 --> 00:51:59.109
And there, it touches on the fact of what
you just mentioned before, which is, well,

00:51:59.109 --> 00:52:02.520
if I've got a bunch of these things, and I
have DOIs, how do I know that the quality

00:52:02.520 --> 00:52:06.520
of that data is high, or not just a bunch
of data that is meaningless?

00:52:06.520 --> 00:52:07.910
So what are the bars there?

00:52:07.910 --> 00:52:11.420
And I think the application there is, how
do we do that?

00:52:11.420 --> 00:52:15.150
And I don't think we know yet, and we have
to look at it.

00:52:15.150 --> 00:52:19.460
But I think it's really important that NIH
think about the way that they set up incentives

00:52:19.460 --> 00:52:24.640
for that, and they also have policies in place
which are the-- I guess the stick part of

00:52:24.640 --> 00:52:30.369
the carrot and the stick, where there is some
level of ensuring that the community follows

00:52:30.369 --> 00:52:31.369
some rules.

00:52:31.369 --> 00:52:35.450
But I think it would be wrong for NIH to simply
use the stick all the time.

00:52:35.450 --> 00:52:40.140
It would be good to have both of those in
play, in part because that's the nature of

00:52:40.140 --> 00:52:44.320
the way systems work, and not all humans play
fair.

00:52:44.320 --> 00:52:48.890
So I think it would be really good to see
if we could have a combination of those.

00:52:48.890 --> 00:52:52.460
That's just sort of my broad, general view
of what I'm seeing with the culture change.

00:52:52.460 --> 00:52:55.040
I'm certainly seeing it changing more and
more.

00:52:55.040 --> 00:52:56.230
Every year, I see changes.

00:52:56.230 --> 00:53:00.660
And that's, I think, a good thing towards
open.

00:53:00.660 --> 00:53:03.980
I would agree with you on that.

00:53:03.980 --> 00:53:05.930
I think there is a culture change that is
happening.

00:53:05.930 --> 00:53:11.910
It's not happening overnight, but it is slowly
percolating up and becoming more common.

00:53:11.910 --> 00:53:16.680
Another question, and this is going to some
of the comments-- or some of the remarks you

00:53:16.680 --> 00:53:22.230
made regarding bioCADDIE and other sort of
efforts.

00:53:22.230 --> 00:53:26.089
The question revolves around researchers being
able to find the most appropriate resources

00:53:26.089 --> 00:53:27.089
for their needs.

00:53:27.089 --> 00:53:32.630
And I would assume that that is both with
respect to data and the tools and software.

00:53:32.630 --> 00:53:38.790
Could you comment further on other sort of
works that bioCADDIE or other groups that

00:53:38.790 --> 00:53:40.050
you're aware of are doing to sort of help
researchers in this regard?

00:53:40.050 --> 00:53:44.520
aware of are doing to sort of help researchers
in this regard?

00:53:44.520 --> 00:53:45.520
Sure.

00:53:45.520 --> 00:53:47.690
So I think this is a wide-open field.

00:53:47.690 --> 00:53:52.260
So I think bioCADDIE has been doing a lot
of work in this area, to try and find ways

00:53:52.260 --> 00:53:53.609
of indexing resources.

00:53:53.609 --> 00:53:56.380
And I think we're in the early days of that.

00:53:56.380 --> 00:54:00.810
I'm not going to go into the details of bioCADDIE
here, in part because I know it to a certain

00:54:00.810 --> 00:54:04.880
degree, but I think the question is more relevant
broadly.

00:54:04.880 --> 00:54:10.810
I think there's a lot of efforts happening,
both in industry as well as in research.

00:54:10.810 --> 00:54:14.690
I've certainly seen it within BD2K, that there
are different slants on the way that you might

00:54:14.690 --> 00:54:17.860
want to look at discoverability.

00:54:17.860 --> 00:54:22.190
It partly depends on what you want to find
and how you want to find it.

00:54:22.190 --> 00:54:25.140
Some things are more discoverable than others
because of their metadata.

00:54:25.140 --> 00:54:26.140
Sometimes they're different kind of objects.

00:54:26.140 --> 00:54:30.060
Sometimes you're going to find software, sometimes
you don't.

00:54:30.060 --> 00:54:35.960
So there are multiple research and commercial
efforts underway.

00:54:35.960 --> 00:54:40.610
And I think what we need to do is to sort
of do some bake-offs to see, how do they work

00:54:40.610 --> 00:54:43.880
against some of the data and tools that we
have?

00:54:43.880 --> 00:54:47.480
How does that work?

00:54:47.480 --> 00:54:50.730
And I think we're just starting to begin that
whole process.

00:54:50.730 --> 00:54:59.050
I'll give you an example of some of the work
I've done with Zenodo, over at CERN in Europe.

00:54:59.050 --> 00:55:04.720
They're looking at how that they-- both a
repository, but also access points for data

00:55:04.720 --> 00:55:05.720
and tools.

00:55:05.720 --> 00:55:08.450
How can they search for that?

00:55:08.450 --> 00:55:10.400
So what's the right way to do that?

00:55:10.400 --> 00:55:13.099
And then once you do find it, how do you connect
it with a service?

00:55:13.099 --> 00:55:17.099
How do you do something with it once you've
actually found it?

00:55:17.099 --> 00:55:21.660
I think there is that, and then an obvious
one, of course, is, Google's really good at

00:55:21.660 --> 00:55:22.910
this kind of stuff.

00:55:22.910 --> 00:55:27.220
And a lot of their work around schema.org,
which is now being incorporated into bioschema.org,

00:55:27.220 --> 00:55:33.760
I think is another effort to look at, how
do you leverage ways that methodologies related

00:55:33.760 --> 00:55:38.579
to discoverability can be used in more appropriate
ways for biomedicine?

00:55:38.579 --> 00:55:43.650
So for example, I know that bioCADDIE has
been moving towards incorporating a lot of

00:55:43.650 --> 00:55:48.640
that work within its own DataMed component.

00:55:48.640 --> 00:55:57.730
So I think it's a wide-open field, and it's
both got industry as well as researchers across

00:55:57.730 --> 00:55:58.730
the world looking at this.

00:55:58.730 --> 00:56:03.369
And I think we're at the stage where we really
need to start probing into, what are the different

00:56:03.369 --> 00:56:04.369
resources that are available?

00:56:04.369 --> 00:56:05.760
How do they do it differently?

00:56:05.760 --> 00:56:08.950
And is it-- does it impact what you're searching
for?

00:56:08.950 --> 00:56:14.100
If a different-- does it matter which type
of digital asset you're searching for?

00:56:14.100 --> 00:56:17.530
I think that's currently where we've been
thinking at NIH.

00:56:17.530 --> 00:56:21.550
And I think the work that we've seen from
bioCADDIE has been helping us better understand

00:56:21.550 --> 00:56:24.040
the way that the community is thinking about
it.

00:56:24.040 --> 00:56:28.930
And as bioCADDIE has been looking at indexing
various data sets, we're sort of seeing the

00:56:28.930 --> 00:56:32.560
outcome of those, and saying, well, that one
worked, and this one didn't work so well.

00:56:32.560 --> 00:56:36.540
So that helps us feed back into the system
to say what we need to actually do.

00:56:36.540 --> 00:56:41.410
It's a pretty general answer, but I think
that's just generally where we're at the moment.

00:56:41.410 --> 00:56:42.560
Fair enough.

00:56:42.560 --> 00:56:48.410
I think that there are, obviously, lots of
BD2K-specific projects that are also looking

00:56:48.410 --> 00:56:51.320
at indexing from different perspectives.

00:56:51.320 --> 00:56:55.650
I know that there's ones from UCLA, and there
are others throughout the different centers

00:56:55.650 --> 00:57:00.320
of excellence, so-- And I think the good thing
is that they're all coming together under

00:57:00.320 --> 00:57:01.339
one umbrella.

00:57:01.339 --> 00:57:09.630
So actually, there was a question that was
posed, which is talking about FAIR framework,

00:57:09.630 --> 00:57:12.480
and the working groups around that.

00:57:12.480 --> 00:57:16.040
I think I can answer that for you, in this
particular case.

00:57:16.040 --> 00:57:21.380
Someone asked, how do you go about finding
out more about FAIR and joining the working

00:57:21.380 --> 00:57:22.380
group?

00:57:22.380 --> 00:57:31.020
There is a Commons framework working groups
that actually addresses FAIR and other aspects.

00:57:31.020 --> 00:57:36.750
And we'll make that information available
more broadly to the community after this talk,

00:57:36.750 --> 00:57:44.440
if individuals are interested in finding out
more about FAIR and the various working groups

00:57:44.440 --> 00:57:46.490
that Vivien has mentioned.

00:57:46.490 --> 00:57:51.300
Yeah, that-- I would add, Alex, there, that
Valentina di Francesco over at NHGRI is the

00:57:51.300 --> 00:57:52.300
contact person for this-- from the NIH perspective,
too.

00:57:52.300 --> 00:57:53.300
Yes.

00:57:53.300 --> 00:57:54.300
So one last question before we go.

00:57:54.300 --> 00:58:04.540
This has to do, again, with the platform and
the idea of collaboration.

00:58:04.540 --> 00:58:10.240
Do you envision any types of collaborative
tools to support active collaboration, or

00:58:10.240 --> 00:58:17.180
being more proactive in forging collaborations
within the cloud-- or not within the cloud,

00:58:17.180 --> 00:58:18.640
within the Commons?

00:58:18.640 --> 00:58:23.000
So things like being able to communicate,
find researchers-

00:58:23.000 --> 00:58:24.000
Yes.

00:58:24.000 --> 00:58:25.640
So I-- yes, I do.

00:58:25.640 --> 00:58:30.140
And I think that layer is often not seen by--
often by NIH.

00:58:30.140 --> 00:58:35.200
But for example, figshare, that does a lot
of these connections, [INAUDIBLE].

00:58:35.200 --> 00:58:38.140
There are groups out there that have pieces
of information that would be really useful

00:58:38.140 --> 00:58:41.460
to connect with in this environment, not just
the data.

00:58:41.460 --> 00:58:45.930
There are also people that are developing
collaborative-- tools for collaboration that

00:58:45.930 --> 00:58:53.550
allow connections, data to tools through services,
but also ways to do other things, like linking

00:58:53.550 --> 00:58:59.369
to scholarly research and scholarly papers,
journals, publications, et cetera.

00:58:59.369 --> 00:59:03.720
And there are several groups looking at that,
and have actually developed some platforms

00:59:03.720 --> 00:59:05.000
in this area.

00:59:05.000 --> 00:59:09.720
And I think we need to look at what that will
be, because one, the way that they're developing

00:59:09.720 --> 00:59:16.050
those collaborative platforms, so to speak,
are a lot easier for biologists to use.

00:59:16.050 --> 00:59:18.550
They don't-- there's a lower learning curve.

00:59:18.550 --> 00:59:19.550
curve.

00:59:19.550 --> 00:59:22.849
And the cloud, to some extent, if it's being
used, is hidden in the background.

00:59:22.849 --> 00:59:28.359
But it's still being used, but it's a whole
lot easier for the people to connect to it.

00:59:28.359 --> 00:59:33.069
If you think about it, like, for example,
I gave the example of AirBnB.

00:59:33.069 --> 00:59:34.250
You can see it immediately.

00:59:34.250 --> 00:59:36.820
You just see the picture of what you want,
and then you can connect to it.

00:59:36.820 --> 00:59:40.030
All the pieces that happen underneath, behind
it, the data, the cloud, all the rest of it,

00:59:40.030 --> 00:59:41.030
is visible to you.

00:59:41.030 --> 00:59:43.590
But it means that anyone can use that platform.

00:59:43.590 --> 00:59:46.480
And I think that's where the collaboration
pieces fit into play with some of these tools.

00:59:46.480 --> 00:59:52.160
They're going to enable more and more people
to use these environments, and it's going

00:59:52.160 --> 00:59:57.980
to enable them to do science faster, and at
scale, with lower levels of computing capability.

00:59:57.980 --> 01:00:00.570
And that's fine, because many biologists don't
have that.

01:00:00.570 --> 01:00:04.210
What we have to do is, we're right at the
start of figuring out, if this is what we're

01:00:04.210 --> 01:00:06.010
looking at as the ecosystem, what do we need
to test?

01:00:06.010 --> 01:00:09.599
And there's a lot of technology we need to
test before we can get to the place where

01:00:09.599 --> 01:00:13.280
it becomes smooth transactions in that environment.

01:00:13.280 --> 01:00:14.920
Hope that helps.

